---
title: Plain rice
description: Lesson 0.1. A pot of rice by the absorption method, with nothing in the way.
servings: 4
time: 35 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, basics]
lesson: "0.1"
technique: absorption method
status: draft
---

> Technique: the absorption method. Rice and a measured amount of
> water go in the pot together and the lid goes on. When the water has
> all been taken up, the rice is done. Nothing is drained, so the
> measuring is the whole skill.

Put @long-grain rice{200%g} in a #sieve{} and rinse it under cold running water, moving it with your hand, until the water runs clear. Drain it well.

> Why rinse: it washes off the loose starch on the surface of the
> grains. Left on, that starch takes up water during cooking and glues
> the grains together.
-- source: "Rules for Cooking Rice", America's Test Kitchen, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/how_tos/5709-rules-for-cooking-rice

Put the rice in a #saucepan{}(with a tight lid) with @water{350%ml} and @salt{1/2%tsp}. Bring to a boil over high heat.

As soon as it boils, turn the heat as low as it goes and put the lid on. Cook for ~{15%minutes}. Do not lift the lid and do not stir.

Take the pan off the heat. Lay a clean tea towel under the lid, cover again, and leave for ~{10%minutes}. Fluff the rice with a fork.

> Why rest under a towel: the towel soaks up the steam that would
> otherwise drip back and make the top layer sticky.
-- source: "Rules for Cooking Rice", America's Test Kitchen, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/how_tos/5709-rules-for-cooking-rice

> Science: a raw grain of rice is hard because its starch is packed
> in dense, partly crystalline granules. Heated in water above about
> 52 °C the granules swell, lose their crystalline order and let out
> amylose. That change is starch gelatinization, and it is what
> cooking rice means. Starch comes as amylose, a linear chain, and
> amylopectin, a branched one; long-grain rice has more amylose and
> cooks up separate, short-grain has less and cooks up sticky. Look
> up: starch gelatinization, amylose, amylopectin.
-- source: Bashir and Aggarwal, "Physicochemical, structural and functional properties of native and irradiated starch: a review", Journal of Food Science and Technology 56(2):513-523, 2019, https://doi.org/10.1007/s13197-018-3530-2 ; Sawyer Hill, "Do You Really Need to Rinse Your Rice?", America's Test Kitchen, 2021-10-26, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/3946-do-you-really-need-to-rinse-your-rice (a rice grower, interviewed; stated, not tested)

> Safety: uncooked rice can carry spores of Bacillus cereus, which
> survive cooking. Eat rice hot, or chill leftovers as quickly as
> possible, ideally within 1 hour, keep them in the fridge, eat them
> within 24 hours, and reheat only once, until steaming hot all the
> way through.
-- source: Food Standards Scotland, "Food storage", https://www.foodstandards.gov.scot/consumers/food-safety/at-home/rice

> Next time, without the recipe: rinsed long-grain rice and one and a
> half times its volume of water. Boil, cover, lowest heat for 15
> minutes, then 10 minutes of rest with the lid on.
-- source: "Steamed White Rice", America's Test Kitchen, 2018-01-08, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/recipes/10613-steamed-white-rice (2 cups rice to 3 cups water)
